          "text": "## Branch I — Occitania, Capetian incorporation and the French Logos\n\nThe Languedoc/Occitan field provides a long branch rather than one moment of language suppression. The Albigensian Crusade begins in 1209 in a region whose political, linguistic and religious relations do not coincide with the later French nation-state. The Treaty of Paris in 1229 marks a major Capetian expansion into the Toulouse field; in 1271 Toulouse enters the royal domain. Political incorporation precedes the later full administrative and educational dominance of French.\n\nThe 1539 Ordinance of Villers-Cotterêts is another ring in that growth. Its legal-administrative language provisions displace Latin in specified official records and strengthen French as a common jurisdictional medium. The historical operation becomes more explicit during the Revolution. Abbé Grégoire's 1794 report treats the persistence of the so-called *patois* as an obstacle to political amalgamation and imagines linguistic unity as part of making one national mass.\n\nThis branch therefore requires several distinct leaves to remain visible:\n\n- 1209 and the crusading-political transformation of the Occitan south;\n- 1229 and 1271 as stages of territorial/jurisdictional incorporation;\n- 1539 as administrative-language standardisation;\n- 1794 as programmatic national-linguistic homogenisation;\n- nineteenth- and twentieth-century schooling and the subsequent social production of French linguistic normativity.\n\nThe word *patois* is itself an archaeology task. Once a situated speech-world is characterised as merely local, backward or administratively deficient, its politically produced marginality can later be cited as evidence of its intrinsic inadequacy. Name, policy and changed social world then form a loop.\n\n**Future work:** source the exact Occitan linguistic situation across the crusade and post-crusade centuries; establish the administrative scope of Villers-Cotterêts without retrospective exaggeration; install Grégoire's report as a primary source; trace schooling and local testimony through the nineteenth and twentieth centuries.\n"
