          "end_line": 88,
          "text": "## Branch III — Renaissance transmission: Medici, Ficino, Pico, Visconti, Pacioli and Leonardo\n\nThe Renaissance field is a set of connected but geographically differentiated branches.\n\nIn Florence, Cosimo de' Medici's acquisition of Hermetic texts and his instruction that Marsilio Ficino translate the Hermetica before returning to Plato establish a concrete patronage mechanism for recovered esoteric/philosophical cosmology. Ficino and Pico then sit inside a wider learned field of Platonism, magic and Christian Kabbalah. The historical question is not patronage in the abstract: it is which texts, translations, circles and institutions were enabled by which patrons and what became possible downstream because of that support.\n\nThe Visconti–Orléans line supplies a different mechanism. Valentina Visconti's marriage to Louis I of Orléans transmitted dynastic claims into the French royal field; descendants including Louis XII inherited a claim to Milan that became directly relevant to the Italian Wars. Marriage here is historical infrastructure: persons, claims, households, heraldry, symbols, court practices and political ambitions can travel together.\n\nThe **Minervois Biscione** sighting remains a concrete research leaf rather than an established Visconti conduit. The next work is object-level: identify the exact object/building, date it, establish patronage and renovation history, trace the iconographic/heraldic route, and only then determine whether Visconti/Orléans transmission explains its presence. The serpent-child image remains available as Mytheme once the object itself is securely recovered.\n\nPacioli and Leonardo give the branch its mathematical hinge. Double-entry practice predates Pacioli, while the 1494 *Summa* provides the earliest printed accounting treatise and a major Venetian exposition. *De divina proportione* belongs to mathematical proportion and geometrical form, with Leonardo's illustrations/association and a 1509 printed edition. The same historical milieu therefore keeps quantitative account and qualitative/symbolic geometry in close relation before later disciplinary differentiations separate them more sharply.\n\nThe project's phrase for this is **the undivided hinge of mathesis**. A related research hypothesis is the **differential occultation of qualitative mathesis**: not the disappearance of mathematics, but the unequal institutional futures of calculative and symbolic/qualitative mathematical practices.\n\nGeography remains explicit: Florence carries Medici/Ficino/Pico; Venice carries commerce and accounting; Milan carries Sforza/Pacioli/Leonardo and the geometry/proportion field.\n"
        }
      ],
