          "text": "## Branch VI — Italy: literary Logos becoming national infrastructure\n\nItalian gives a relatively clear case where a literary language helps prepare a national field before unified state administration can make that language broadly ordinary. Tuscan/Florentine literary prestige has a long pre-unification history. Alessandro Manzoni's nineteenth-century intervention deliberately privileges living Florentine and, after unification, his role in the 1868 language commission joins literary norm to state educational policy.\n\nThe branch can therefore be followed as:\n\n```text\nliterary Logos → political nation → educational standardisation\n```\n\nThe important leaves are literary prestige, post-1861 administrative needs, Manzoni's proposals, schooling, literacy, regional speech and the later emergence of a much more broadly shared spoken Italian.\n"
