          "text": "## Branch X — Abrahamic Word, Law, people and political order\n\nF. E. Peters' comparative work opens a wider Abrahamic branch:\n\n> **revelatory Word ↔ normative Law ↔ collective identity ↔ territorial/political order**\n\nThe geometries remain distinct. Judaism joins Torah/halakhic life to communal continuity across changing relations to divine, communal and host-state law. Christianity universalises across *ethnē* and languages, makes Logos itself central, sustains Latin as a transregional learned/ritual medium, and later gives vernacular scripture a major role in Reformation and national histories. Islam privileges Qur'anic Arabic within a multilingual *umma* while *sharia* and *fiqh* develop across multiple empires, schools and local orders.\n\nThis branch is valuable precisely because the same four terms do not coincide in the same way. The history can show alternative distributions of Word, Law, people and place rather than reading every Abrahamic formation as a proto-nation-state.\n"
